From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:08:13 +0300
Subject: nv30: signed 8-bit vertex attributes normalized in the vertex program
 (NV30_VTX_SNORM8)

NV3x/NV4x and the RSX have no signed 8-bit vertex type, so u_vbuf converts GL_BYTE normalized
attributes to floats on the CPU at every draw, over the draw's whole vertex range, static
buffers included. 0033's lookup table made the conversion cheaper, but in the profile of
2026-10-19 (Mesa ps3.476) it was still 18.1% of Sauerbraten's CPU (generic_run under
renderbatches: the world's normals and tangents, R8G8B8_SNORM and R8G8B8A8_SNORM), and the
CPU reads of the VRAM buffers it does also give every VRAM buffer a system memory copy
(NV30_SYSMEM_COPY's hint). QuakeSpasm's model normals are the same format.

With NV30_VTX_SNORM8=1 (off by default) nv30 reports R8_SNORM to R8G8B8A8_SNORM as vertex
formats, so u_vbuf leaves them alone. The GPU fetches them as U8_USCALED (libgcm's UB256:
each byte as a float 0..255) and four instructions at the start of the vertex program give
what util_format's unpack gives, MAX2(-1.0f, (float)(c * (1.0f/0x7f))):

   s = SGE(v, 128); t = MAD(s, -256, v); t = MUL(t, 1/127); t = MAX(t, -1)

The fetch is always four bytes (U8_USCALED x4): the RSX raises graph exception 0x106 for
U8_USCALED with 3 components (Sauerbraten's R8G8B8_SNORM normals wedged the GPU 2 s into its
demo, 2026-10-19), RPCS3 takes UB256 as size 4 only, and nouveau's own table has U8_USCALED
only as R8G8B8A8_USCALED. MUL and MAX work on the attribute's components; for a format of
fewer than four a fifth instruction moves (0, 0, 0, 1) into the others, so the bytes after
the attribute that the fetch also reads never reach the program. That needs four bytes of
room in the vertex from the attribute on (src_offset % stride + 4 <= stride); a 1 to 3 byte
attribute packed at the very end of its vertex is converted to float by nv30 instead, as for
formats the GPU can't fetch. The program then reads the temp instead of the input
(tgsi_src); each instruction reads at most one input and one constant, as the hardware
requires. The inputs this applies to are a
key of 3 bits per input (component count) in the vertex elements state, kept to the inputs
the program declares; a program is translated again when its key changes, as for clip planes
(binding elements with another key marks the program dirty), and the key is part of 0039's
shader cache key. Inline vertices (vbo_fifo) carry the bytes as they are (translate copies an
element whose output format is its input format), constant attributes (stride 0) are emitted
as their raw bytes, so every path hands the program the same values. A program that indexes its
inputs can't be redirected and goes to the draw module, which fetches SNORM8 itself.

No conversion on the CPU and no system memory copies for those buffers; the cost is four vertex
program instructions per such attribute and vertex (five below four components). The pixels are
the same if the RSX's multiply rounds as IEEE single precision does (at most one ulp off
otherwise).

Checked on the host (PS3 tree, tools/run-tests.sh mesa-vtx-snorm8): the prologue's arithmetic
against util_format's generated unpack functions for the four formats, every byte in every
component, whatever bytes follow the attribute, bit for bit; and nvfx_vertprog.c's output, with
and without the prologue, run on an NV40 vertex program interpreter on 3000 vertices for 2
programs and 5 keys (inputs read with swizzles, negated, as abs, twice in one instruction): the
same outputs bit for bit, four instructions more per converted input (five below four
components), the same input mask.

Forwarded: no

